WEBVTT

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:10.303
Hi there.

00:00:10.303 --> 00:00:14.863
Welcome to this video where we are going
to learn about social media influencers.

00:00:15.583 --> 00:00:18.383
The goal of the video is 
to help you understand

00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:21.375
what influencers are
and what motivates them.

00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:24.975
Firstly, we will look at 
what an influencer is.

00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:28.783
Then we will look at 
some examples of influencers.

00:00:29.423 --> 00:00:35.215
Finally, we will discuss what the driving 
forces behind the influencers activities are.

00:00:39.407 --> 00:00:42.399
What is a social media influencer?

00:00:42.927 --> 00:00:46.847
Social media influencers are people
who have built a significant following

00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:49.999
on social media platforms
and have the ability to affect

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:53.887
their audiences opinions, 
behaviors, and purchasing decisions.

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:57.087
What defines an influencer?

00:00:57.087 --> 00:01:01.967
They create regular content,
videos, photos, posts, streams

00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:06.767
around  specific topics or lifestyles, 
and their followers trust their opinions

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:11.119
or enjoy their personality enough 
to engage with and act on their recommendations.

00:01:11.455 --> 00:01:16.607
Influencers typically partner with brands 
to promote products or services to their audience.

00:01:22.191 --> 00:01:27.600
Among common categories of products 
and services promoted by influencers are:

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.415
beauty and fashion, gaming and technology,
fitness and wellness, food and cooking,

00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:36.735
travel and lifestyle, 
education and how to content,

00:01:37.135 --> 00:01:43.599
comedy and entertainment,
parenting and family life.

00:01:46.223 --> 00:01:49.423
Influencers typically make money
through sponsored posts

00:01:49.455 --> 00:01:52.463
because brands pay them 
to feature products.

00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:57.615
Influencers can also earn commission money
on sales for the products they promote.

00:01:58.255 --> 00:02:02.223
Influencers can also sometimes
sell their own merchandise or products.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.455
Let us have an imaginary influencer, Mary.

00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:09.743
She is paid by global manufacturers
of popcorn and headphones.

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:15.055
Thus, each second day she publishes a post
on the newest products demonstrating

00:02:15.055 --> 00:02:21.503
how she eats popcorn and watches movies 
using particular headphones.

00:02:23.711 --> 00:02:26.399
There are several things to be aware of.

00:02:26.399 --> 00:02:29.391
Unrealistic standards 
and self-esteem issues.

00:02:30.095 --> 00:02:34.655
Influencers often present heavily edited,
curated versions of their lives

00:02:34.655 --> 00:02:39.695
that can make people feel inadequate about 
their own appearance, lifestyle, or achievements.

00:02:40.366 --> 00:02:45.646
This constant comparison can lead to anxiety, 
depression, and body image issues.

00:02:45.646 --> 00:02:51.406
For example, showing only the most expensive 
shoes, clothes or sports equipment.

00:02:51.406 --> 00:02:54.414
Hidden advertising and manipulation.

00:02:54.686 --> 00:02:58.766
Many influencers promote products
without clearly disclosing sponsorships,

00:02:58.846 --> 00:03:02.526
making it hard for people to know
whether they are genuine recommendations

00:03:02.526 --> 00:03:05.486
or just to make money
for promoting products.

00:03:05.486 --> 00:03:09.326
People may feel pressure to buy things
they don't need or can't afford.

00:03:10.014 --> 00:03:14.446
For example, an influencer might promote
a particular game, saying it is the best

00:03:14.446 --> 00:03:18.526
game ever while secretly being paid
by the game company to promote it.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.198
Dangerous trends and challenges.

00:03:23.198 --> 00:03:27.038
Some influencers promote 
risky behaviors, dangerous challenges,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:32.110
or harmful products like extreme diets,
unregulated supplements, or potentially

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.918
dangerous stunts without 
considering the impact on followers.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:43.030
For example, an influencer can say that one 
ice cream per day helps with energy and figure.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.790
Privacy erosion.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:49.950
Influencer culture makes it seem normal
to overshare personal information online.

00:03:50.638 --> 00:03:53.998
People may mimic this behavior
without understanding the long term

00:03:53.998 --> 00:03:57.118
consequences for their privacy
and digital footprint.

00:03:57.838 --> 00:04:01.358
For example, the influencer shows
birthmarks on the body,

00:04:01.438 --> 00:04:05.118
bedroom details, and even breakfast
routines of their relatives.

00:04:05.918 --> 00:04:07.790
Parasocial relationships.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.550
Teens can develop one sided
emotional attachments to influencers

00:04:11.758 --> 00:04:15.278
feeling like their friends when
the influencer doesn't know they exist.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:20.558
For example, the influencer posts
each late evening inviting to share

00:04:20.558 --> 00:04:24.510
and like the evening routine, 
calling the viewer my friend.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:28.270
Inappropriate content and values.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.790
Some influencers
promote materialism, shallow values,

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:34.830
or inappropriate content
for young audiences.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.598
Others may exhibit problematic behavior,

00:04:37.598 --> 00:04:40.590
drama conflicts, questionable ethics

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.598
that people might view 
as normal or aspirational.

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:46.878
For example, the influencer posts a video

00:04:46.878 --> 00:04:49.870
of their unfriendly conflict
with a salesperson.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.398
Scams and exploitation.

00:04:52.398 --> 00:04:54.958
People may encounter 
fake giveaways, phishing schemes,

00:04:54.958 --> 00:04:58.510
or influencers who exploit 
their audience financially.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.478
Some predators also 
pose as influencers to gain trust.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.550
For example, the influencer recommends
to click on all links under the post.

00:05:08.078 --> 00:05:10.990
Distorted career expectations.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.110
The glamorized influencer
lifestyle can give people

00:05:14.158 --> 00:05:17.150
unrealistic ideas about work success,

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:21.229
and how easy it is 
to make it online,

00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:24.429
potentially affecting their 
academic motivation or career planning.

00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:29.597
For example, an influencer may tell you
that you do not need to work at school

00:05:29.677 --> 00:05:34.477
because you can just earn money traveling 
the world and posting about your experiences.

00:05:35.693 --> 00:05:37.885
So in this short video, we learned

00:05:37.885 --> 00:05:41.693
what an influencer is and looked
at some examples of influencers.

00:05:42.205 --> 00:05:48.093
Finally, we have discussed what the driving 
force is behind the influencers activities.

00:05:48.301 --> 00:05:49.869
Thank you for watching.

00:05:49.869 --> 00:05:52.813
Do not forget to watch
other videos in our series.